Keep the video side across a state load of the running title

A state load (System::serialize) shut the whole system down and built it
again, the GPU and its renderer included: on an iPhone that was most of a
4.3 s load (every cached pipeline built again, the driver pipeline cache read
again), and on the host 290 of a 300 ms rewind restore was that teardown.

A state is only ever loaded into its own title (LoadStateBuffer refuses
another's), and the renderer depends on the system only through the memory
system, the PICA core the GPU owns, the custom texture manager and the
timing. So with System::SetKeepVideoAcrossLoad, a deserializing Shutdown keeps
the GPU, the custom texture manager and the memory system (whose bookkeeping
MemorySystem::ResetForLoad returns to a new one's; the memories' bytes are
all written by the load), Init builds everything else as before and gives the
kept GPU the new timing (GPU::KeepAcrossLoad: its vblank event registered there
by name before the state's queue is read, never scheduled twice), and once the
state's PICA is loaded every register, shader setup and table is marked dirty
(GPU::MarkAllDirtyAfterLoad, what RecreateRenderer does). retro_unserialize and
the rewind restore set it.

Measured on the host (Tools/3ds-bench rewind): restore 290 -> 101 ms
(Craftus Reloaded), 450-500 -> 100 ms (Majora's Mask 3D). Every frame after a
load, picture and sound, identical with the video kept and rebuilt on Craftus
(0 of 300, for the full state and for the rewind snapshot); on Majora's Mask
3D with the 3DS clock fixed (a probe build), the sound identical kept and
rebuilt (six runs gave two sequences, each produced by both modes: the
variation is the host's, not the change's).

